<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    The install engine. STAGE 3 - no window, no screen, nothing to look at.

    docs/work-notes/plans/plugin-extension/02-implementation.md, Stage 3.

    WHY THIS IS C# AND NOT POWERSHELL, WHICH WAS THE OBVIOUS CHOICE.
    The two things an install actually DOES - find Revit, copy the files - are
    already written in PowerShell and proven: tools\HeronRevit.ps1 and
    tools\deploy-addin.ps1. Rewriting either would be two copies of one rule,
    which R-31 refuses.

    What is NOT written anywhere is the DECIDING: which products, for which
    releases, in what order, what to skip and why, and what to do while Revit
    is still open. That half has to be testable, and a repository worked on
    from Linux cannot run one line of PowerShell.

    So the split is: THIS DECIDES, POWERSHELL ACTS. Everything in here is
    pure enough to run and be tested anywhere; the two adapters that reach
    Windows sit behind IRevitEnvironment and IProductDeployer and are the only
    parts that need the owner's machine.

    net8.0, NOT net8.0-windows, and NOT referencing Heron.Core.

    THE ENGINE IS RELEASE-INDEPENDENT and Heron.Core is not. Heron.Core
    follows HeronTfm, so it is net472 for Revit 2020 and net8.0-windows for
    2025; referencing it would pin this to whichever release happened to be
    building. It was tried on 2026-09-21, for HeronPaths, and the coupling is
    the reason it was undone rather than the MSBuild error it produced.

    WHAT IT WANTED HeronPaths FOR - the Revit Addins folder, to answer
    whether a product is already installed - now comes from
    tools\HeronRevit.ps1 through IRevitEnvironment. That script has to know
    the path anyway, because it runs before any Heron assembly exists on the
    machine, so asking it removes a copy of the rule rather than adding one.

    NO UseWPF here, ever. The moment this needs a window it stops being
    runnable on the machine it is developed on, and every decision it makes
    stops being testable. The window is platform\Heron.Installer.App and
    holds no decisions at all.

    NOT a Revit add-in. It never loads into Revit.exe, so it may use
    System.Text.Json freely - the reason revit\Heron.Bridge\Json.cs is
    hand-written (a serializer in Revit's load context is a known and
    unpleasant class of bug) does not apply here.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <RootNamespace>Heron.Installer</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>Heron.Installer</AssemblyName>
    <Nullable>disable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>disable</ImplicitUsings>

    <!--
      Directory.Build.props drives every other project from RevitVersion and
      maps it to a runtime. This one is release-independent: it installs FOR a
      Revit release without ever loading into one, so it is built once.
    -->
    <HeronTfm>net8.0</HeronTfm>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
